INT. SYNERGY ROOM - FORMER FUNERAL HOME - DAY

A wood-paneled chapel converted into an open-plan collaboration space. The pews are gone but their ghost-marks remain on the carpet. A bean-bag sits where the casket stood. A motivational vinyl decal reads: BRING YOUR WHOLE SELF.

BARB KOWALCZYK, 50s, regional operations lead, lanyard heavy with badges, stands at a whiteboard that still has a hymn board frame around it. She is drawing a funnel. She has been drawing the same funnel for four meetings.

A glass door hisses. A young man hesitates in the doorway, holding a laptop and a succulent in a coffee mug.

BARB
You're early.

NEW HIRE
Is it okay? They said ten.

BARB
Nobody's ever early. It's kind of a red flag.

He steps in. He looks at the ceiling, which has an ornate plaster rosette and a smoke detector blinking green. He looks at the bean-bag.

NEW HIRE
Is that where the--

BARB
We don't say that. We say "the Hub."

NEW HIRE
Right. Sorry.

BARB
Don't be sorry. Be curious.

She pulls a marker cap off with her teeth. The funnel gets another arrow. He sets his succulent on the windowsill and the windowsill, a wide stone ledge, makes a very quiet clack.

NEW HIRE
I'm supposed to shadow someone on onboarding?

BARB
That's me. I'm the someone.

NEW HIRE
Great. I'm really excited.

BARB
You're allowed to be neutral.

NEW HIRE
Okay.

BARB
That was neutral?

NEW HIRE
It was an attempt.

She finally looks at him, a long unhurried look from lanyard to shoes. He holds the laptop to his chest like a hymnal.

BARB
Name.

NEW HIRE
Teodoro. Teo, mostly.

BARB
(writing it on the board, inside the funnel)
Teodoro. And you came to us from?

TEODORO
A mattress start-up. Direct to consumer.

BARB
Did it fold?

TEODORO
Like a mattress.

She does not laugh. She taps the marker on the word Teodoro twice.

BARB
Today you will learn three things. One. Our brand voice. Two. Our values. Three. How to book the Hub.

TEODORO
The Hub is bookable?

BARB
By the hour. There's a calendar. It's color-coded. Green is generative, red is reflective, and gray is--

She stops. She looks at the carpet, where a large gray rectangle has been worn by years of folding chairs.

BARB (CONT'D)
Gray is a sensitive color.

TEODORO
Okay.

BARB
You keep saying okay.

TEODORO
I didn't know that I did.

BARB
Eleven times. I counted.

He opens his laptop to have something to do. It bongs loudly with a cheerful startup chime. Barb flinches, a tiny flinch, then covers it by uncapping a second marker.

BARB (CONT'D)
Turn the volume off. Please. We have a no-chime policy in the Hub.

TEODORO
Is that a value?

BARB
It's a boundary.

He mutes it. A long beat. The smoke detector blinks. Barb writes BOUNDARIES on the whiteboard and underlines it hard enough that the marker squeaks.

TEODORO
Can I ask something? The person I replaced. What happened to them?

BARB
He pivoted.

TEODORO
Where?

BARB
Out.

TEODORO
They said he got promoted.

BARB
(a flat smile)
Out is up, in a sense.

Teodoro nods too many times. Barb watches the nodding. Something in her jaw is working, a small muscle. She caps the marker. Uncaps it.

BARB (CONT'D)
Do you know how many people work here?

TEODORO
Two hundred?

BARB
Forty. Two hundred is the number on the slide.

TEODORO
Why is there a slide?

BARB
There is always a slide.

She picks up a remote and a projector hums to life. A deck appears on the wall above the old altar. It says OUR JOURNEY. Under it, a stock photo of a mountain with a staircase built into it.

TEODORO
That staircase isn't attached to anything.

BARB
It is a metaphor.

TEODORO
It's pointing at a cloud.

BARB
Teodoro.

TEODORO
I'm just saying someone approved that.

Her thumb is white on the remote. She clicks. The next slide: a pie chart, one slice labeled SYNERGY, filling ninety percent of the circle.

BARB
Do you see the large slice?

TEODORO
Yeah.

BARB
That slice is where we live.

TEODORO
What's the other ten percent?

BARB
Compliance. And some tears.

TEODORO
Some what?

BARB
Typo. The slide has a typo.

She clicks again, harder. The clicker snaps in two. A spring pings across the carpet and rolls into the old gray rectangle. They both watch it go.

TEODORO
Do you want me to--

BARB
Do not move.

He doesn't. She walks to the spring, picks it up between two fingers, and holds it up to the light as though it might confess.

BARB (CONT'D)
Four years. I have booked this room for four years. I have put a succulent on every ledge. I have told forty people that the Hub is a safe space. And every Monday I come in and I draw the funnel, and the funnel is the same, and the funnel does nothing, Teodoro. It does nothing!

She hurls the spring at the whiteboard. It ticks off the plastic hymn frame.

TEODORO
(carefully)
Barb, are you okay?

BARB
I am ABSOLUTELY neutral.

She rips the BOUNDARIES page off the wall. There is no page, it is a whiteboard, so she drags her sleeve across the word and smears it into a gray cloud. She turns. Her chest rises and falls. Teodoro has stood, laptop clutched, succulent forgotten.

BARB (CONT'D)
Pull up the calendar.

TEODORO
Now?

BARB
Book the Hub.

TEODORO
For when?

BARB
Right now. Reflective. Make it red.

He types. His fingers shake slightly. She sits on the bean-bag where the casket stood, sinking down with a long sigh of polystyrene beads, and puts the marker cap in her mouth again.

TEODORO
Booked. It's red.

BARB
Good.

She closes her eyes. The projector continues to glow over her, the staircase going up into the cloud.

BARB (CONT'D)
Welcome to the team.
